.TH STRIP 1
.SH NAME
strip \- remove symbols from an executable program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B strip
file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B strip
removes the symbol table from each executable
.I file
named. The program runs the same without it, and takes less space on
the disk, but
.BR nm (1)
and
.BR adb (1)
can no longer show its names. A program stripped is the same, byte for
byte, as one linked with
.BR "cc \-s" .
A program with no symbol table is left as it is.
.PP
strip writes the program without its symbols to a temporary file, and
then copies that back over the original, which keeps its owner, group
and mode.
.SH FILES
.TP 14
/tmp/stp*
the temporary file
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
"not an executable" for an object file or a library, which keeps its
symbols: the link editor needs them. "write error: the file may be
damaged" if the copy back failed part way. The exit status is 1 if a
file could not be stripped, and 0 otherwise.
.SH SEE ALSO
cc(1), ld86(1), nm(1), size(1), adb(1)
.SH HISTORY
A
.B strip
command appeared in Version 1 AT&T UNIX.
.SH AUTHORS
This strip was written for the xenix86-devsys Development System.
